Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 07/12/2024]
 1. Observed-resonance energies are calculated from Ex in ENSDF 2018 and the nuclear Q value.
 2. Observed-resonance strengths are from Haenninen 1984; all their strengths were determined relative to that of Elab = 1394 keV [Ecm = 1360 keV], 
    wg = 1.30 +- 0.25 eV, which agrees with the earlier result of Maripuu 1970 [however, the 36Ar(p,g) strength of Elab = 918 keV in the latter work is 20% lower 
    than our adopted standard value].
 3. Er = 167 keV: Gp is calculated from measured C2S=0.25 of Knoepfle et al. (1974); assumption of Gg=0.1 eV is inconsequential.
 4. Er = 17 - 821 keV [except 167 keV]: since C2S values are not known, they enter as upper-limit contributions; for Er < 440 keV, Gp << Gg: when the lifetime
    is unknown, we choose Gg = 0.1 +- 0.001 eV, where both the mean value and its uncertainty are likely inconsequential; for Er > 440, when the lifetime is 
    unknown: Gp and Gg could have comparable magnitudes, and we choose Gg = 0.1 eV, with a factor of 10 uncertainty.
 5. Er = 743 keV: no uncertainty is reported for the excitation energy; we adopt +- 10 keV. 
 6. Er = 361, 440, 536 keV: although ENSDF master table does not list any lifetimes in their column 3, measured lifetimes are listed in the last [comment]
    column, which were measured by (g,g'); they give Gg values near 0.2 eV, which is larger than the upper-limit Gp values. Therefore, Gg will not matter much
    for their rate contributions.
 7. Direct capture contribution: calculated using measured spectroscopic factors from (3He,d) work of Knoepfle et al. 1974 and (d,n) work of Hagen et al. 1970. 
    Only the former provides results for the most important transition, but the resolution in their spectrum is poor [120 keV]. We assume a factor of 3 uncertainty
    in the total DC S factor.  
 
 
